## 2. Experimental
## 2.1 Materials and instruments
The materials and instruments involved in this work were supplemented in the supporting information.
2.2 The preparation of \( \mathrm{Zr}_{12} \)-adb-H1 conjugates
\(\mathrm{Zr}_{12}\)-adb nanoplate was prepared according to previous report (Dai et al., 2017), except that \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\)adb was used instead of \(2^{\prime}\)-nitro-\([1,1^{\prime}:4^{\prime},1^{\prime\prime}\)-terphenyl]-\(4,4^{\prime\prime}\)-dicarboxylic acid \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{TPDC}-\mathrm{NO}_{2}\right)\) during the synthesis. Initially, \(7.5~\mathrm{mg}\) \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\)adb, \(4.2~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}\), \(10~\mathrm{mL}\) DMF and \(0.75~\mathrm{mL}\) glacial acetic acid were mixed in a vial. Then, the mixture was sonicated for \(10~\mathrm{min}\) to disperse all solids evenly and kept at \(80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(48~\mathrm{h}\). The resultant yellow suspension was collected by centrifugation and washed several times with DMF and THF, respectively. Finally, the yellow product was dried under vacuum at \(50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) to obtain yellow powder for subsequent use.
Before using, the phosphate-terminal hairpin 1 (H1) was annealed at \(95~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in Tris-HCl buffer for \(5~\mathrm{min}\) and then cooled down to ambient temperature slowly. Then, \(300~\upmu\mathrm{L}~100~\upmu\mathrm{M}\) annealed H1 and \(2~\mathrm{mg}\) freshly prepared \(\mathrm{Zr}_{12}\)-adb nanoplates were dispersed in \(1~\mathrm{mL}\) DMF. The mixture was incubated overnight on a shaker at room temperature. In order to reduce electrostatic repulsion between neighboring H1 and get high densities of H1 in the surface of \(\mathrm{Zr}_{12}\)-adb nanoplate, sodium chloride was slowly added into the mixture to a final concentration of \(0.5~\mathrm{M}\). The resultant was washed with ultrapure water and centrifuged at \(5000~\mathrm{rpm}\) for \(10~\mathrm{min}\) to remove free H1. Eventually, the obtained \(\mathrm{Zr}_{12}\)-adb-H1 conjugates were dispersed in \(1~\mathrm{mL}\) ultrapure water for subsequent application.
## 2.3 The fabrication of ECL sensing interface
Prior to modification, the glass carbon electrode (GCE, \(\Phi = 4~\mathrm{mm}\)) was pretreated with the previous method (Zeng et al., 2018; Zhuo et al., 2014). Briefly, the bare GCE was polished with \(0.3~\mathrm{and}~0.05~\upmu\mathrm{m}~\alpha\)-alumina powder followed by
sonication in ethanol and ultrapure water respectively to get a clear mirror-like surface. After that, \(5~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) \(Z\mathbf{r}_{12}\) -adb-H1 conjugates were dropped on the clear surface of GCE and dried in ambient temperature to gain a uniform layer.
## 2.4 Measurement Procedure
Firstly, MUC1 aptamer, hairpin 2 (H2), hairpin 3 (H3), Fc-labeled hairpin 4 (H4-Fc) were annealed at \(95~^{\mathrm{o}}\mathrm{C}\) for \(5~\mathrm{min}\) and then cooled down slowly to ambient temperature, respectively. After that, \(20~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of the mixture solution containing \(2~\upmu\mathrm{M}\) aptamer, \(2~\upmu\mathrm{M}\) H2, \(2~\upmu\mathrm{M}\) H3 and various concentrations of MUC1 was incubated for \(30~\mathrm{min}\) on a shaker at room temperature to form abundant H2-H3 duplexes. Then the mixture was dropped on the modified electrode (\(Z\mathbf{r}_{12}\)-adb-H1/GCE) and maintained \(2~\mathrm{hours}\) at room temperature. After being rinsed with PBS solution (0.1 M, \(\mathrm{pH}=7.4\)), the ECL response of biosensor was measured in \(2~\mathrm{mL}\) PBS solution containing \(2~\mathrm{mM}\) TPrA with the scanning potential from \(0~\mathrm{V}\) to \(1~\mathrm{V}\), and the scanning rate was \(0.3~\mathrm{V/s}\).
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1 SEM and XRD characterization of \(Z\mathbf{r}_{12}\)-adb and \(Z\mathbf{r}_{12}\)-adb-H1
In order to characterize the prepared \(Z\mathbf{r}_{12}\)-adb and \(Z\mathbf{r}_{12}\)-adb-H1, we used scanning electron microscopy (SEM) to observe their morphologies and sizes. As shown in Figs. 1A and 1B, \(Z\mathbf{r}_{12}\)-adb exhibited a round nanoplate shape with a diameter of \(100{-}200~\mathrm{nm}\) and a thickness of about \(20\mathrm{nm}\). Additionally, as exhibited in Fig. 1C, powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) was used to characterize the structure of \(Z\mathbf{r}_{12}\)-adb, which showed that \(Z\mathbf{r}_{12}\)-adb adopted \(Z\mathbf{r}_{12}\)-TPDC (TPDC = terphenyl dicarboxylate) topology with the PXRD pattern matching that of \(Z\mathbf{r}_{12}\)-TPDC (Dai et al., 2017). All of
the above characterizations demonstrated the successful preparation of \( \mathrm{Zr}_{12} \)-adb nanoplate.
To prove the successful synthesis of \( \mathrm{Zr}_{12} \)-adb-H1, the zeta potential of \( \mathrm{Zr}_{12} \)-adb and \( \mathrm{Zr}_{12} \)-adb-H1 were measured respectively by a dynamic laser light scattering. As shown in Fig. S1A, the zeta potential of \( \mathrm{Zr}_{12} \)-adb was positive with an average of 20.9 mV. However, due to H1 was negative, the zeta potential of \( \mathrm{Zr}_{12} \)-adb-H1 was negative with an average of -12.9 mV, which demonstrated that the H1 was coordinated to the vacant locus of \( \mathrm{Zr}_{12} \) clusters and the \( \mathrm{Zr}_{12} \)-adb-H1 was prepared successfully. Then, the SEM and EDS mapping were employed to characterize \( \mathrm{Zr}_{12} \)-adb-H1. As depicted in Fig. S2A, \( \mathrm{Zr}_{12} \)-adb-H1 exhibited a shape of round nanoplates and EDS mapping revealed that C, O, Zr, and P were uniformly distributed in the synthesized \( \mathrm{Zr}_{12} \)-adb-H1 nanoplate, and EDS spectrum of the \( \mathrm{Zr}_{12} \)-adb-H1 showed the peaks of C, O, Zr and P elements (Fig. S2F), which suggested that the \( \mathrm{Zr}_{12} \)-adb-H1 was prepared successfully.
